:root{--bg:#fbfaf7;--fg:#1d2430;--muted:#5a6472;--rule:#d9d5cc;--grey-soft:#ebe9e3;--amx:#5b3fa0;--amx-soft:#e6def6;
  --sans:"IBM Plex Sans",system-ui,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",sans-serif;--mono:"IBM Plex Mono",ui-monospace,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color-scheme:light;}
body{background:var(--bg);color:var(--fg);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;margin:0;padding:28px 16px 56px;}
main{max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;}
h1{font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;margin:0 0 6px;}
h2{font-size:20px;font-weight:600;margin:40px 0 10px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding-top:18px;}
h3{font-size:16px;font-weight:600;margin:22px 0 6px;}
p{margin:0 0 12px;max-width:72ch;} ul{padding-left:22px;margin:0 0 12px;} li{margin:0 0 6px;max-width:72ch;}
.meta{color:var(--muted);font-size:13px;margin-bottom:22px;}
.short{background:var(--grey-soft);border-left:4px solid var(--fg);padding:12px 16px;margin:0 0 20px;}
.short p{margin:0 0 8px;} .short p:last-child{margin:0;}
.short .lbl{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted);font-weight:600;display:block;margin-bottom:6px;}
code{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;background:var(--grey-soft);padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;}
table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13.5px;margin:0 0 14px;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;}
.tbl{overflow-x:auto;margin:0 0 14px;}
th,td{text-align:left;vertical-align:top;padding:5px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);}
th{font-weight:600;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--muted);} td.n,th.n{text-align:right;}
.fig{margin:16px 0 20px;overflow-x:auto;} .fig svg{display:block;max-width:100%;min-width:720px;height:auto;}
.fig .cap{font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:6px;max-width:74ch;} .fig .take{font-size:14px;font-weight:500;margin-top:6px;}
.insuf{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:13px;color:#8a2d2d;}
.gloss dt{font-weight:600;margin-top:8px;} .gloss dd{margin:0 0 4px 0;max-width:72ch;}
@media (max-width:480px){h1{font-size:24px;} body{font-size:14.5px;}}
</style></head><body><main>
<h1>Muse Glimmer with the kv_mul-16 AMX kernels: measured</h1>
<div class="meta">Branch jhan-amx-kvmul16 at 5061623a34 (tron main 6198824246 plus the kernel generalization), binary runtron.g16 built on delphi-3bda. Runs 2026-10-16T23:34:12Z to 2026-10-17T00:18:34Z UTC on our half of delphi-3bda. 36 of 36 planned runs finished. Written for jhan. Generated by exec/glimmer-20261016/gen_page.py from the raw result files.</div>
<div class="short"><span class="lbl">Short version</span>
<p>With the AMX attention kernels generalized to 16 query heads per KV head, Muse Glimmer 30B decodes 4.4 % to 15.2 % (4 cells) faster per user in the production configuration (FPGA attention on the 13 full layers), and 6.1 % to 21.2 % (4 cells) faster with all 52 layers on the CPU.</p>
<p>At the nightly CI configuration (Glimmer tp4, 2 engines, 8 users per machine, prompt 1024, the nightly's own harness) the branch decodes 4.6 % faster per user than the installed nightly build (78.69 to 82.27 tokens/s, mean of 3 passes per arm, the base arm within +0.0 % of the nightly's own row of 78.69), and the time to first token is 3.4 % shorter (3,780 to 3,651 ms).</p>
<p>The attention path counters confirm that the kernel ran where expected in every decode forward (34 of 36 per-layer checks match, the 2 others are prompt forwards at prompt 2048 explained in section 4a), and the llama-8b cell with the unchanged 4-head path decodes 5.2 % faster with AMX on than off (1 cell).</p>
</div>
<h2>Words used here</h2><dl class="gloss">
<dt>tron, runtron, engine</dt><dd>tron is the inference program under test. runtron is its command-line tool; every run here is one runtron stream-generate-text call. An engine is one tron process serving one copy of the model; every run here uses one engine.</dd>
<dt>delphi-3bda, our half, CI</dt><dd>delphi-3bda is the CI test machine (two CPU sockets, eight FPGA cards). Our half is socket 1 with the FPGA cards 90, 93, b9 and bc. CI is the nightly continuous-integration test that owns the machine at night.</dd>
<dt>AMX, AVX</dt><dd>Intel CPU instruction sets. AMX multiplies 16-row tiles; the AMX kernel scores one dense KV page per call. AVX is the 512-bit vector set the software attention loop (the dotter) uses for every other page.</dd>
<dt>AMX on / AMX off, the kill switch</dt><dd>The same binary in both arms. AMX off sets TRON_AMX_DISABLE=1, the kill switch: the AMX probe answers no once per process, so no query is packed for the kernel and every page goes through the AVX loop. Both arms set TRON_ATTN_STATS=1.</dd>
<dt>TRON_AMX_DISPATCH</dt><dd>The CMake option that compiles the AMX kernels into the binary. The run binary has it on.</dd>
<dt>K, V, KV cache, KV head</dt><dd>K (key) and V (value) are the two per-token tensors attention reads back at every step; they are stored in the KV cache, one 64-token page at a time. A KV head is one 128-dim K/V pair shared by a group of query heads.</dd>
<dt>kv_mul</dt><dd>Query heads per KV head (grouped-query attention). llama-3.1-8b has 4; Glimmer has 32 query heads over 2 KV heads, so 16.</dd>
<dt>Muse Glimmer 30B</dt><dd>The production ingested plugin ingested-meta-models--Muse-Glimmer-30B (tp2 and tp4 variants; an ingested plugin is a model whose C++ code tron&#x27;s ingest compiler generated). 52 layers: 39 use a 2048-token sliding window and always run on the CPU, 13 are full-attention layers that go to the FPGA card when USE_HW_ATTN is unset.</dd>
<dt>FPGA attention / CPU attention</dt><dd>attn=fpga: USE_HW_ATTN unset, the model default (13 layers on the card). attn=cpu: USE_HW_ATTN=0, all 52 layers in software.</dd>
<dt>tp2, tp4</dt><dd>Tensor-parallel width: the model split over 2 or 4 FPGA cards. The tp2 runs use cards 90 and 93, the tp4 runs cards 90, 93, b9 and bc.</dd>
<dt>cell, regression cell</dt><dd>One combination of model, tp, users, prompt length and attention mode; each cell ran with AMX on and AMX off. The regression cell is the llama-3.1-8b cell, included to show that the unchanged 4-head path still behaves as before.</dd>
<dt>prompt 1024 / 2048</dt><dd>The prompt length in tokens (runtron --prompt-length); runtron adds one start token, so the prompts have 1025 and 2049 tokens. Every user is asked for 256 tokens (-l 256 --dont-stop). runtron&#x27;s rate line counts 253 response tokens, and the decode-like forward count is 256.</dd>
<dt>decode TPS per user</dt><dd>runtron&#x27;s &#x27;average tok/s&#x27; of one user&#x27;s generation phase (counted response tokens divided by the generation time), averaged over the 8 users of a run, then over the repetitions.</dd>
<dt>prefill time</dt><dd>runtron&#x27;s &#x27;Parsing the prompt took S s&#x27; of the slowest user of a run (all 8 prompts are parsed together), then averaged over repetitions.</dd>
<dt>attention path stats, exit report</dt><dd>TRON_ATTN_STATS=1 makes tron print an exit report: per layer, how many K tokens were scored by the AMX kernel, the AVX loop and the FPGA, separately for decode-like forwards (every job of the forward has a listener, the object that receives the generated token) and prompt-or-mixed forwards (at least one job has none).</dd>
<dt>K tokens</dt><dd>Tokens of the KV cache read by one attention pass, counted once per KV head (the software scale). The exit report counts FPGA K tokens once per query pass for all KV heads at once; this page multiplies them by the number of KV heads (2 for Glimmer) so that every count on the page has one unit.</dd>
<dt>forward</dt><dd>One pass of the model over all layers for one batch of jobs. A decode step of the 8 users is one forward.</dd>
<dt>platformd</dt><dd>The service manager that runs the production rinzler units (rinzler is the production server) on delphi-3bda. The campaign asks it to stop idle serving before the runs and to start it again afterwards.</dd>
<dt>rc</dt><dd>The exit status of a test binary; 0 means it passed.</dd>
<dt>delta</dt><dd>AMX on relative to AMX off, in percent. For a rate a positive delta is a gain. For a time a negative delta is a gain.</dd>
<dt>deb</dt><dd>The Debian package of tron that the nightly installs on delphi-3bda (/opt/positron). The nightly deb is main. The branch deb is the same build recipe from jhan-amx-kvmul16.</dd>
<dt>TTFT</dt><dd>Time to first token, in ms. The CI harness measures it from sending the request to the first streamed token, so it holds the prompt parse of the 4 users sharing one engine.</dd>
<dt>sd</dt><dd>Standard deviation: the spread of the values around their mean, in the same unit as the values.</dd>
<dt>paired t</dt><dd>The paired t statistic over the pass pairs of two arms: the mean of the per-pass differences divided by its standard error. With 3 pairs a value beyond +-4.3 is unlikely to come from pass-to-pass spread alone (5 % two-sided).</dd>
<dt>prefill proxy</dt><dd>The CI harness&#x27;s own prefill rate: 1024 prompt tokens divided by TTFT, in tokens/s.</dd>
<dt>ShareGPT set, chat template</dt><dd>ShareGPT is a public collection of recorded chat conversations. The CI harness cuts each one to the prompt length with its own tokenizer. The chat template is the model&#x27;s own wrapping of the messages into one token stream.</dd>
<dt>test proxy</dt><dd>The Caddy reverse proxy in front of the engines on delphi-3bda. It spreads the users&#x27; requests over the engines.</dd>
</dl>
<h2>1. What was measured</h2>
<p>One binary (runtron.g16, branch jhan-amx-kvmul16 at 5061623a34, preset native with BUILD_INGEST_MODELS and TRON_AMX_DISPATCH on) ran 9 cells. Each cell ran with AMX on and then with AMX off, 2 repetitions (the full plan). Every run used 8 users and our half of delphi-3bda (socket 1). Production serving was stopped through platformd for the duration and started again afterwards.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>cell</th><th>model</th><th>tp</th><th>attention</th><th class="n">prompt</th><th class="n">runs AMX on / off</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp2-8u-p1024-fpga</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp2</td><td>2</td><td>FPGA on the full layers</td><td class="n">1024</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp2-8u-p2048-fpga</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp2</td><td>2</td><td>FPGA on the full layers</td><td class="n">2048</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp4-8u-p1024-fpga</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp4</td><td>4</td><td>FPGA on the full layers</td><td class="n">1024</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp4-8u-p2048-fpga</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp4</td><td>4</td><td>FPGA on the full layers</td><td class="n">2048</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp2-8u-p1024-cpu</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp2</td><td>2</td><td>CPU, all layers</td><td class="n">1024</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp2-8u-p2048-cpu</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp2</td><td>2</td><td>CPU, all layers</td><td class="n">2048</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp4-8u-p1024-cpu</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp4</td><td>4</td><td>CPU, all layers</td><td class="n">1024</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>glm-tp4-8u-p2048-cpu</code></td><td>Glimmer-30B tp4</td><td>4</td><td>CPU, all layers</td><td class="n">2048</td><td class="n">2 / 2</td></tr>
