<rect x="0" y="602" width="12" height="12" fill="#5b3fa0"/><text x="16" y="612" class="ax">AMX kernel</text>
<rect x="200" y="602" width="12" height="12" fill="#0f6e8c"/><text x="216" y="612" class="ax">AVX software loop</text>
<rect x="400" y="602" width="12" height="12" fill="#c2601a"/><text x="416" y="612" class="ax">FPGA card</text>
<text x="0" y="590" class="ax">column height = share of the layer's K tokens scored by each path (slices below 2 px are drawn 2 px tall)</text>
</svg><div class="take">With AMX on, the 39 sliding-window layers are violet (AMX) and the 13 full layers stay orange (FPGA) in the production configuration. With AMX off every CPU layer is teal (AVX).</div><div class="cap">The tp4 cells are not drawn, because their per-layer counts equal the tp2 counts exactly (the attention work of one engine process does not depend on the tensor-parallel width). Full layers of Glimmer as seen in the exit reports (layers with FPGA K tokens): 3, 7, 11, 15, 19, 23, 27, 31, 35, 39, 43, 47, 51. In decode forwards the full layers keep a thin teal slice. It is the step's own token plus the 0 to 3 earlier tokens whose copy to the card is not complete yet, 2.5 K tokens per user per KV head per step on average (the card's prefix advances in groups of 4 positions) [h/tron/scheduler/full.hpp:2899-2910]. The rest of the layer goes to the card.</div></div>
<h3>Per-layer checks</h3>
<p>36 checks (9 cells x 2 arms x 2 forward classes, repetition 1): 34 match the expectation. The expectation per model and attention mode:</p><ul>
<li>Glimmer with FPGA attention: AMX K tokens on the 39 sliding layers only, FPGA K tokens on the 13 full layers only.</li>
<li>Glimmer with CPU attention: AMX K tokens on all 52 layers.</li>
<li>llama-3.1-8b: AMX K tokens on all 32 layers.</li>
<li>AMX off: no AMX K tokens on any layer.</li></ul>
<p>The checks that differ, all in prompt forwards of the prompt-2048 cells with FPGA attention:</p><ul>
<li><code>glm-tp2-8u-p2048-fpga</code>, AMX on, prompt forwards: AMX K tokens on 52 layers (expected 39), FPGA K tokens on 13 layers (expected 13), both on 13 layers (expected 0).</li>
<li><code>glm-tp4-8u-p2048-fpga</code>, AMX on, prompt forwards: AMX K tokens on 52 layers (expected 39), FPGA K tokens on 13 layers (expected 13), both on 13 layers (expected 0).</li>
</ul><p>In those forwards the 13 full layers scored part of their K tokens on the CPU with the AMX kernel next to the FPGA (the column 'full: AMX' in the table below). The decode forwards of the same runs match the expectation. Section 4a explains the mechanism.</p>
<h3>K tokens by layer kind (Glimmer, repetition 1, software scale)</h3>
