</table></div><p>Sliding layers = the 39 layers with window 2048 (software only). Full layers = the 13 FPGA candidates. In the CPU-attention cells every layer is in software, so the full layers show AMX and AVX instead of FPGA. The FPGA column carries the KV-head factor, so the shares equal those of the campaign's own report (attn-stats-compare.md). The tp2 and tp4 rows of a cell are identical, because the attention work of one engine process does not depend on the tensor-parallel width.</p>
<h3>4a. Why the full layers score some prompt K tokens on the CPU, and why the AMX kernel appears there only at prompt 2048</h3>
<p>Derived from the tron source on 2026-10-17 by a 7-agent read-and-refute workflow. The script exec/glimmer-20261016/glimmer_model.py reproduces every AMX, AVX and FPGA K-token count of both prompt lengths from this model. One zero-token visit count (empty visits in the prompt-2048 decode class) stays open and carries no K tokens.</p>
<p>Words used here:</p><ul>
<li>chunk = the 128 prompt tokens of one user that one forward processes.</li>
<li>shard = a 1024-token block of K/V in the card's memory.</li>
<li>KV book = the host-side K/V storage of one token-tree edge, 1600 tokens by default. The token tree is tron's structure of all sequences in flight. An edge is one run of tokens in it.</li>
<li>the walker = the scheduler code that plans a forward by walking the token tree and deciding, per shard, whether the card may score it.</li>
<li>visible range = the span of K positions one query may see; it ends at the query's own position.</li>
<li>dense page = a full 64-token page that the AMX kernel may serve.</li></ul><ul>
<li>A prompt is fed in chunks of 128 tokens per user per forward. A 1025-token prompt takes 8 prompt forwards and a 2049-token prompt takes 16 [h/libtron.hpp:197-204; src/tron/generation/context.cpp:40-42].</li>
<li>The card scores only the shards whose copy had completed when the forward was planned. The chunk being processed is therefore always scored on the CPU, and the first chunk (positions 0 to 127) has no card work at all [h/tron/scheduler/full.hpp:1979-1996, 2862-2916; src/tron/models/ranged_mask.cpp:63-83].</li>
<li>The pages of the current chunk are still being written during the forward. Each query token of the chunk also has its own visible range. A page in that state is never dense, so the AVX loop scores it [h/tron/models/self_attention.hpp:1711-1722, 1881-1892; src/tron/models/ranged_mask.cpp:42-48, 64-66]. That is the AVX share of the full layers in every prompt forward, and the reason the AMX kernel does not appear on the full layers at prompt 1024.</li>
<li>At prompt 2048 the first KV book of every user (1600 tokens) is full at the 13th chunk (positions 1536 to 1663). The token tree starts a new edge with a new book at position 1536, and the partial shard that spans the split moves to the new edge. In that one forward the walker refuses the moved shard, because its copy ends at position 1535, below the new edge's base [full.hpp:54-69, 942-951, 1022-1049, 1178-1184, 2909-2916; h/tron/scheduler/token_tree.hpp:316-330].</li>
<li>So in that forward all 8 users get card work only for positions 0 to 1023 and score positions 1024 to 1535 on the CPU. Those positions are complete pages from earlier forwards. The first user has 8 dense pages there. The other seven users share only the start token with the first prompt, branch off after it into their own books, and so have a page grid offset by one token: 7 dense pages plus two partial ones [h/tron/scheduler/token_tree.hpp:636-651; full.hpp:947-949]. The dense pages take the AMX kernel, the partial ones the AVX loop. This is the only AMX work on the full layers.</li>
<li>Arithmetic: the card deficit per full layer is 8 users x 128 queries x 512 tokens = 524,288 query-token pairs. On the software scale (2 KV heads) that is 1,048,576 K tokens: 933,888 scored by the AMX kernel (the logged per-layer AMX count) plus 114,688 scored by the AVX loop for the partial pages (contained in the logged AVX count of 2,228,210, whose remainder is the current-chunk work). The counts are identical across repetitions, tp2 and tp4, and AMX on and off (with the kill switch the 933,888 are added to the AVX count). The log line 'KV cache footprint: 1.03 GB in 13 books' appears in every prompt-2048 run and in no prompt-1024 run. 13 books x 1600 tokens x 53,248 bytes is the first crossing of the 1 GiB print step [h/tron/models/kv_cache.hpp:560-580, 1351].</li>
</ul><p>What this means for the measurement: the pattern is structural, not a race. It costs the prompt-2048 cells one forward's worth of extra CPU attention on the 13 full layers in both arms. Not verified in a log line: the refusal of the moved shard itself. Two measurements would settle it: a debug print at the partial-shard push (edge base, copy end, pushed or not), or a rerun with TRON_BOOK_INITIAL_TOKENS=2176 and TRON_BOOK_MAX_TOKENS=2176, which should give AMX 0 on the full layers at prompt 2048.</p>
<h2>5. Attention time per decode forward</h2>
<p>From the exit report's cycle counters, decode-like forwards only. Wall time per forward is the whole model step. Busy time per forward per worker is the attention workers' busy cycles divided by that repetition's number of active attention workers and by the forwards. The busy cycles cover both software passes and the join at the end of a layer, including the time a worker waited there for the others [h/tron/models/attn_stats.hpp:230-237]. The join wait share column says how much of the busy time was that wait.</p>
