</table></div><p>Busy share of wall = busy ms per forward per worker divided by wall ms per forward. It is the share of a decode step that the average attention worker spent inside its attention jobs, join wait included. It is an average over workers, and a step waits for its slowest worker, so it is a guide to the attention cost of a step, not a bound on what a faster kernel can remove. A worker count of the form '23 / 24' means the two repetitions ran with different counts.</p>
<h2>6. The code change and its unit tests</h2>
<p>The three kernels (pack_q_group_128x4, qk_rowmajor_128x4, weights_times_v_128x4) are function templates on the group size, instantiated for 4 and 16 heads. The gate shape_ok accepts head size 128 with kv_mul 4 or 16. The score kernel's transposing store gained three more lane variants for 16 rows (new shuffle selectors, not just a loop bound). Buffers were already geometry-sized. Every name is kept. 363 insertions, 164 deletions over six files.</p>
<p>Preservation of the 4-head path, from a compare of the -O3 objects of amx_attn.cpp (main 6198824246 against the branch, clang 19.1.7 on claude-box with the mirrored nix headers, exec/results/glimmer-20261016/local-obj-compare/):</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>kernel</th><th class="n">instructions main</th><th class="n">4-head instantiation</th><th class="n">16-head instantiation</th><th class="n">lane shuffles (vshuff) 4 / 16 heads</th><th class="n">tile multiplies (tdpbf16ps) 4 / 16 heads</th><th>4-head vs main</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>pack_q_group_128x4</code></td><td class="n">509</td><td class="n">509</td><td class="n">1285</td><td class="n">0 / 0</td><td class="n">0 / 0</td><td>instruction-identical</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>qk_rowmajor_128x4</code></td><td class="n">204</td><td class="n">204</td><td class="n">207</td><td class="n">0 / 40</td><td class="n">16 / 16</td><td>instruction-identical</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>weights_times_v_128x4</code></td><td class="n">121</td><td class="n">121</td><td class="n">206</td><td class="n">0 / 0</td><td class="n">16 / 16</td><td>differs (thread-local scratch access only; see the note)</td></tr>
</table></div><p>The 4-head weights-times-V kernel differs from main only in how it addresses its thread-local scratch (the template's thread_local uses the general-dynamic access model where main's used local-dynamic). Both relax to the direct local-exec form when linked into the executable: the linked runtron.g16 on delphi-3bda contains no __tls_get_addr call. The campaign's own preservation check in tests.sh could not find the templated symbols by demangled name and reports n/a; the table above is the evidence.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>test on delphi-3bda (real AMX, fake device)</th><th class="n">assertions</th><th class="n">rc</th><th>note</th></tr>
<tr><td>t_amx_numerics</td><td class="n">17424</td><td class="n">0</td><td>env=[] skips=0 All tests passed (17424 assertions in 4 test cases)</td></tr>
<tr><td>t_amx_dispatch_dtype</td><td class="n">5756</td><td class="n">0</td><td>env=[] skips=0 All tests passed (5756 assertions in 1 test case)</td></tr>
<tr><td>t_llama_unit</td><td class="n">265452</td><td class="n">0</td><td>env=[] skips=0 All tests passed (265452 assertions in 67 test cases)</td></tr>
<tr><td>t_amx_numerics-killswitch</td><td class="n">4110</td><td class="n">0</td><td>env=[TRON_AMX_DISABLE=1] skips=2 AMX unavailable on this host - QK precision check skipped AMX unavailable on this host - PV precision check skipped All tests passed (4110 assertions in 4 test cases)</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p>The kill-switch row runs t_amx_numerics with TRON_AMX_DISABLE=1. Its two skips are the AMX-only precision sections, as designed. t_amx_numerics has sections for 4 and 16 heads (kernel against the AVX dotter, with the documented bf16 rounding of the softmax weights). t_amx_dispatch_dtype checks that each of the three kernel entry points is called with the group size of the model.</p>
<h2>7. What the design page got wrong</h2>
<p>Section 4 of muse-glimmer-amx.html listed the changes before the implementation. Three points differ from what was needed (the design page marks four table rows 'Corrected'; its shape_ok row and its apply_dense_amx_page row record the same call-site change).</p><ul>
<li>The three kernel call sites in self_attention.hpp do change: they pass the group size as a template argument (the gate sites read shape_ok unchanged).</li>
<li>The score store is not a 'full 16x16 transpose, writing 16 score rows' by a loop bound (the design page's wording before its 2026-10-17 update). Rows 4 to 15 need their own shuffle selectors in stages 3 and 4 of the 16 x 16 score-tile transpose, the two _mm512_shuffle_f32x4 stages [src/tron/kernels/amx_attn.cpp:150-161], so the kernel has four lane variants of the store.</li>
<li>t_llama_unit also changes: its requires-probes (compile-time checks that a kernel overload exists) name the 4-head instantiation.</li>
</ul><p>Models affected: at head size 128, kv_mul 16 is Muse Glimmer 30B and its permuted twin only. The kv_mul-8 models (llama-70b family, qwen-3-32b, qwen-3-30b-moe) stay refused by the gate.</p>
<h2>8. Caveats and open items</h2><ul>
<li><b>Repetitions (sections 2 to 5).</b> 2 per arm. The tables list both, and the largest difference between the two repetitions of one arm is 0.41 tokens/s per user. A delta smaller than that difference would not be a result; every decode delta here is far larger. Section 9 has 3 passes per arm.</li>
<li><b>Our half only (sections 2 to 5).</b> One engine on socket 1 with 8 users. The nightly serves the same users behind 2 or 4 engines, so absolute TPS differs from the CI rows. Section 9 holds the measurement at the CI configuration.</li>
<li><b>CI round comparators (section 9).</b> The nightly reference is one run of 80 requests. The base arm's decode TPS is within +0.0 % of it, but the base arm's TTFT is 2.3 % higher (3,780 vs 3,697 ms). So the TTFT gain is -3.4 % against the base arm and -1.2 % against the nightly row. The decode gain is +4.6 % against the base arm and +4.6 % against the nightly row.</li>
<li><b>Single binary.</b> AMX off is the kill switch in the branch binary (the AMX code is compiled in, and the AMX probe answers no once per process), not a build without the AMX code. main itself cannot give an AMX-on arm for Glimmer, because its gate refuses kv_mul 16. Whether the kill-switch arm equals a build without the AMX code on the CPU-attention cells is <span class="insuf">Insufficient data</span>. At the CI cell (section 9) the kill switch and the nightly deb (main, whose gate refuses Glimmer, 0 AMX busy cycles in 3 of 3 base passes) differ by -0.1 % decode TPS (78.69 to 78.60 tokens/s per user, paired t -0.4 over 3 passes), inside the spread over passes. That covers one cell with FPGA attention and a main build whose AMX code is compiled in but refused at the gate. A TRON_AMX_DISPATCH=OFF build on the CPU-attention cells is still the measurement that settles the rest.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9. The nightly CI configuration</h2>
<p>The nightly CI runs Glimmer tp4 with 8 users per machine. This round ran that exact cell on the whole of delphi-3bda with the nightly's own harness.</p>
<p>The cell:</p><ul>
<li>2 engines of 4 cards each, started by platformd.</li>
<li>The 8 users reach the engines through the test proxy. The harness pools the 8 users over the 2 engines. The per-pass table below shows how many requests each engine served.</li>
<li>The CI harness is systems_test scripts/perf.py. Each prompt is one conversation of the ShareGPT set, wrapped in the model's chat template and cut to 1,024 tokens. Every request generates 1,536 tokens. One pass is 10 rounds of 8 requests, 80 requests in all. The decode rate is taken between generated tokens 896 and 1,024.</li>
</ul><p>The arms:</p><ul>
<li><b>base</b> = the nightly deb as installed, tron 2026.10.17-3751511d. That is main, whose AMX gate refuses Glimmer.</li>
<li><b>AMX off</b> = the branch deb, tron 2026.10.17-5061623a, with the kill switch TRON_AMX_DISABLE=1 in /opt/positron/user/config.env.</li>
<li><b>AMX on</b> = the branch deb, tron 2026.10.17-5061623a, without the kill switch.</li>
</ul><p>Every arm runs FPGA attention on the 13 full layers, as the nightly does. 9 passes ran in all. One pass is one harness run of the cell.</p>
