<p>base to amxoff: decode 78.69 to 78.60 tokens/s per user (-0.1 %), TTFT 3,780 to 3,781 ms (+0.0 %), paired t over 3 passes = -0.4.</p>
<p>amxoff to amxon: decode 78.60 to 82.27 tokens/s per user (+4.7 %), TTFT 3,781 to 3,651 ms (-3.4 %), paired t over 3 passes = 13.1.</p>
<p>base to amxon: decode 78.69 to 82.27 tokens/s per user (+4.6 %), TTFT 3,780 to 3,651 ms (-3.4 %), paired t over 3 passes = 33.1.</p>
<p>The prompt token counts come from the server's usage field of each response. The harness cuts every conversation to 1,024 tokens with its own tokenizer. That count includes a llama-style header per message. So no request exceeds the 1,024-token budget. The server counts 925 to 1,023 prompt tokens per request (mean 992). Why the server counts fewer is not verified. Hypothesis: the server's own chat template tokenizes the message headers differently. A comparison of the two tokenizations of one request would settle it.</p>
<p>The requests per engine column counts the requests each of the 2 engines served in the pass. The AMX busy cycles are a 20-second sample of the EXE.AMX_BUSY counter (an Intel core event that counts cycles with AMX work in flight) on both engine processes. The sample runs before each benchmark pass, while 4 short probe requests execute with the same binary and environment as the pass. A count of 0 shows that the engines ran no AMX instruction during that sample.</p>
<p>Scripts and raw results: exec/glimci-20261017/ (campaign.sh, dut.sh, st_ci_perf.py, summarize.py) and exec/results/glimci-20261017/&lt;pass&gt;/ (perf.json with every request, driver.log, journal-prompts.txt).</p>
